WEBVTT

00:00:00.480 --> 00:00:04.265
Manual and degraded operation can preserve a critical service when automation,

00:00:04.265 --> 00:00:07.000
communications, or operator visibility is unavailable.

00:00:07.000 --> 00:00:10.990
The fallback may involve local control, reduced throughput, alternate equipment,

00:00:10.990 --> 00:00:14.000
physical rounds, temporary communications, or a controlled process shutdown.

00:00:14.000 --> 00:00:17.920
Manual operation is realistic only when the process was designed for it and people can

00:00:17.920 --> 00:00:19.000
perform it safely.

00:00:19.000 --> 00:00:22.990
Some modern systems lack local controls, accessible measurements, staffing,

00:00:22.990 --> 00:00:25.000
or response time for manual control.

00:00:25.000 --> 00:00:28.950
A plan should never assume that operators can replace fast automated logic by

00:00:28.950 --> 00:00:30.000
improvisation.

00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:34.610
Define entry conditions, authority, minimum staffing, equipment limits, safety checks,

00:00:34.610 --> 00:00:37.995
communications, recordkeeping, and exit criteria.

00:00:37.995 --> 00:00:42.030
Degraded mode may change alarm coverage, interlocks, quality assurance,

00:00:42.030 --> 00:00:46.185
environmental monitoring, or emergency response, so accepted limits must be explicit.

00:00:46.185 --> 00:00:50.200
Provide paper procedures and offline documentation for situations when normal systems are

00:00:50.200 --> 00:00:51.000
unavailable.

00:00:51.000 --> 00:00:54.435
Include current diagrams, operating ranges, contact lists, check sheets,

00:00:54.435 --> 00:00:57.945
startup and shutdown sequences, and locations of tools and spares.

00:00:57.945 --> 00:01:01.000
Protect sensitive copies while keeping them accessible during an outage.

00:01:01.000 --> 00:01:04.220
Train operators and maintenance personnel through safe exercises.

00:01:04.220 --> 00:01:07.990
Consider shift coverage, fatigue, travel restrictions, vendor availability,

00:01:07.990 --> 00:01:10.000
and how long a manual mode can be sustained.

00:01:10.000 --> 00:01:13.985
A fallback that works for thirty minutes may not work for two days.

00:01:13.985 --> 00:01:16.000
Test assumptions and capture lessons.

00:01:16.000 --> 00:01:18.260
If manual operation is unsafe or unrealistic,

00:01:18.260 --> 00:01:22.895
continuity planning should emphasize redundancy, rapid replacement, alternate service,

00:01:22.895 --> 00:01:23.995
or safe shutdown.

00:01:23.995 --> 00:01:27.970
Honest limits are more valuable than a reassuring procedure that cannot be executed.
